  <qti-item-body>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="show_interaction" outcome-identifier="INTERACTION_VISIBILITY" show-hide="show" class="qti-margin-0">
      <qti-content-body>
        <p>Which sentence uses &quot;paragon&quot; correctly?</p>
    <qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="RESPONSE" shuffle="true" max-choices="1">
      <qti-simple-choice identifier="A">The thrift store&apos;s paragon of a winter coat was missing one sleeve and half its buttons, but at that price nobody minded much.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="B">Ilya was the office&apos;s paragon in the sense that he was its newest hire, still learning where the printer paper and coffee filters were kept.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="C">The museum called the shard a paragon simply because it was the only piece of the amphora to survive the kiln fire intact.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="D">The kennel club keeps a bronze cast of one champion&apos;s stance as the paragon of the breed, and judges set every contender beside it.</qti-simple-choice>
    </qti-choice-interaction>
      </qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>

    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-A" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! A paragon is a model of excellence, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because a coat missing a sleeve and half its buttons is a bargain with flaws — the opposite of the perfected standard the word names.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-B" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! A paragon is a model of excellence, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because being the newest hire measures arrival time, not achievement — novelty sets no standard for anyone to be judged against.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-C" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! A paragon is a model of excellence, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because surviving the kiln fire makes the shard rare, and rarity alone is luck — a paragon sets the standard by its quality, not by its survival.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-D" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Correct! A paragon is the model of excellence others are measured against, so it&apos;s the correct answer because the champion&apos;s cast stance is literally the standard: judges set every contender beside it.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
  </qti-item-body>
